{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat AGENTS.md acceptance/README.md apps/docs/lib/conformance.mjs'","aggregated_output":"# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n# Release acceptance\n\nFrom the repository root, run the checker tests and record the four Vitest configurations.\n\n```sh\nnode --test acceptance/test/*.test.mjs\nnode acceptance/run.mjs unit\nnode acceptance/run.mjs browser\nnode acceptance/run.mjs dist\nnode acceptance/run.mjs dist-browser\nnode acceptance/check.mjs --release --write --results \\\n  acceptance/results/unit.json acceptance/results/browser.json \\\n  acceptance/results/dist.json acceptance/results/dist-browser.json\n```\n\nBuild the packages before the browser and distribution configurations. Each wrapper run writes Vitest JSON and a neighbouring `*.provenance.json` with the Git commit, dirty flag, tree fingerprint, result hash and hashes of every cited test file. The sidecar records a working-tree change during Vitest and the release checker rejects that run. The generated `acceptance/report.md` does not affect the fingerprint. Supply the result paths to the checker and keep their sidecars beside them. For a filtered run, use `--output` to keep the full configuration’s result intact.\n\nThe checker prints provenance, the matrix and the sources of each result. It writes [report.md](report.md) only with `--write`. Without results, it checks citations and shows static coverage and any cited packed size measurements. An omitted result leaves its assertions unverified. By default the checker can display historical reports, including archived absolute paths that carry a full repository-relative suffix.\n\nUse `--roots <directory>…` for result paths relative to additional roots such as `tests/dist` or `packages/react`. Paths that match more than one cited file are structural errors.\n\nStructural errors exit 1. In `--release` mode, results must have provenance that matches the working tree. Every cell must be `pass` or `not applicable`. An owner can explicitly waive a `not run` or `cannot be verified here` cell by adding `\"waivers\": { \"<chart type>\": { \"reason\": \"…\", \"grantedBy\": \"…\" } }` to its criterion. The waiver appears in the report and leaves the original status visible.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| pass | All cited assertions passed and measured targets were met. |\n| fail | A cited assertion or suite failed, or a measured target was exceeded. |\n| weak | Partial evidence leaves a documented gap. |\n| no evidence | The criterion applies and has no relevant evidence. |\n| not run | A cited assertion is missing or skipped, or the cell awaits a valid measurement. |\n| not recorded for this revision | Its only answers come from runs or a size record whose provenance is another tree or pack. |\n| cannot be verified here | The criterion requires an environment or rehearsal outside the supplied runs. |\n| not applicable | The matrix records why the criterion does not apply. |\n\n## Browser engines\n\nThe browser configuration runs on the engine `AXYLO_ENGINE` names: `chromium` (system Chrome, the default), `firefox` or `webkit` (Playwright's own builds; WebKit is the engine, not Safari). Each engine gets its own result file:\n\n```sh\nnode acceptance/run.mjs browser                        # acceptance/results/browser.json\nAXYLO_ENGINE=firefox node acceptance/run.mjs browser   # acceptance/results/browser-firefox.json\nAXYLO_ENGINE=webkit node acceptance/run.mjs browser    # acceptance/results/browser-webkit.json\n```\n\nThe harness records `meta.engine` and `meta.engineVersion` on every test, and the sidecar records the engine asked for and the version the results declare. The checker refuses a result file that mixes engines or disagrees with its sidecar. A browser citation takes an optional `engine` (default `chromium`) and is answered only by results of that engine; a pass on one engine never stands for another. A test that reported itself not run on an engine (`meta.notRun`) is `not run` with that reason. Results without engine records predate the switch and count as Chrome; in a run on another engine, a test without a record (the docs and distribution suites launch Chrome themselves) counts for no engine.\n\n## Reading the checker from code\n\n`check()` (acceptance/check.mjs) returns the Markdown `report` and `table`, and the same content as data: `rows` (per criterion its `id`, `statement` and, per chart type, `status`, `reasons` and `reports`, each with `file`, `label`, `source`, `results`, `engine`, `engineVersion` and `provenanceError`) and `runs`. `STATUSES` and `CHART_TYPES` are exported beside it. acceptance/provenance.mjs exports the configurations (`CONFIGS`, `CONFIGURATIONS`), `ENGINES`, `engineLabel()`, `defaultResultFile()` and `resultFiles()`, the default result file of every configuration and engine.\n\n## Review and cite evidence\n\nUse the spec ID and short wording. Section 3.6 cells use `T-<rule>-<chart type>`. Give every excluded chart type a `notApplicable` reason. Compact covers line, area and bar variants where the rule applies.\n\nUse `evidence` for the checks a cell needs, `gaps` for the unverified part when those checks are incomplete, and `weak` for supporting citations with a specific `reason`. Use `limits` to describe the scope of a result, `cannotVerify` for environmental requirements outside the supplied runs, and `pending` for work awaiting a valid run.\n\nEach citation specifies a repository-relative `file` and a `kind` of `unit`, `property`, `browser`, `size`, `benchmark`, `document` or `manual rehearsal`. A test citation gives its exact Vitest `title` or a `prefix` of a describe title when all reported cases are required. A generated title may include its source template as `registration`. Use `ancestors` to cite a specific suite when titles repeat. The checker refuses citations of `.fails`, `.todo` and `.skip` and requires every matching result to pass.\n\nA suite whose cases the source scan cannot list (an `it.each` over rows computed at run time, such as DX10's per-example renders) is cited by its describe `prefix` with `atLeast`: the citation passes when every reported test under the prefix passed, none was skipped, the titles the scan does know are among them, and at least that many distinct tests passed. The `kind` must fit the file: `browser` for `*.browser.test.*` files, `unit` and `property` for the others. A title the scan finds only through a template, or only under an `if`, `else`, `&&`, `||` or `?:`, needs a run that shows it: a supplied result of its file or the `EVIDENCE.json` beside it. Without either the report lists a warning; a result or `EVIDENCE.json` that covers the file but lacks the title is a structural error. A `size` citation counts only when the record names the tarball integrity of the pack this tree makes (`npm pack`, as `scripts/pack.mjs` packs); the targets are `SIZE_TARGETS` in `scripts/size.mjs`. A run whose provenance sidecar does not match the tree, in any mode, and in a release a run without a sidecar, leaves its cells `not recorded for this revision`.\n\nFor a describe suite shared across chart types, add a chart-specific `casePrefix` and the expected number of distinct `cases`. The checker requires all statically known cases and the expected number of passing current registrations. A failed archived result within that scope still appears in the report. Release checks also require matching provenance.\n\nA `size` citation names the budgeted `entries` in the packed size artifact. Historical benchmarks carry a `workload` and optional `history`. Documents, manual rehearsals and provisional benchmark workloads need separate review.\n\nThe archived four-configuration review includes [review notes](review.json) with the prior status baseline, failure causes and the browser helpers checked by negative controls.\n/**\n * The data of the conformance page (spec A9), read when the site is built, so the page cannot say more than\n * its sources do:\n *\n * - which WCAG 2.2 success criteria the specification cites, and in which of its criterion rows\n *   (docs/02-product-spec.md; a row cites \"WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7\" in its last column, spec A9);\n * - what the acceptance checker reports for those rows: `check()` of acceptance/check.mjs, in release mode,\n *   over the result files acceptance/run.mjs writes (acceptance/results/<config>[-<engine>].json, named by\n *   provenance.mjs), so a result counts only when its recorded provenance matches the working tree;\n * - which engine each result came from: the checker's record of each report, or for a result that predates\n *   the engine record, the engine its default result file runs on (provenance.mjs `resultFiles()`).\n *\n * Nothing here judges evidence: a criterion without a counted result is \"not recorded\", and when the checker\n * refuses its matrix nothing is counted at all. Checker output this module cannot read stops the build,\n * rather than being shown as something it is not.\n */\nimport fs from 'node:fs';\nimport path from 'node:path';\nimport { splitRow } from './markdown.mjs';\nimport { wcag } from '../data/wcag.mjs';\nimport { check, CHART_TYPES, STATUSES } from '../../../acceptance/check.mjs';\nimport { ENGINES, engineLabel, resultFiles as defaultResultFiles } from '../../../acceptance/provenance.mjs';\n\nconst SC = /\\d\\.\\d{1,2}\\.\\d{1,2}\\b/g;\n/**\n * What a report can say of the tests it answered: their results, or 'not recorded' when its provenance is\n * another tree or pack, which the checker gives only with a `provenanceError` (README, \"Reading the checker\").\n */\nconst RESULTS = ['pass', 'fail', 'skipped', 'not recorded'];\n\nconst bySc = (a, b) => {\n  const x = a.split('.').map(Number);\n  const y = b.split('.').map(Number);\n  return x[0] - y[0] || x[1] - y[1] || x[2] - y[2];\n};\n\n/**\n * Success criterion → the specification's rows that cite it, each with its criterion text (Markdown). A row\n * cites \"WCAG x.y.z\" in its last column (spec A9); a mention elsewhere in the row is not a citation.\n */\nexport function citedCriteria(spec) {\n  const cited = new Map();\n  for (const line of spec.split(/\\r?\\n/)) {\n    if (!line.startsWith('|')) continue;\n    const cells = splitRow(line);\n    const id = cells[0].replace(/[*_`]/g, '');\n    if (!/^[A-Z]+\\d+$/.test(id)) continue;\n    for (const cell of cells.slice(-1)) {\n      for (const m of cell.matchAll(/WCAG((?:[\\s,]+(?:and\\s+)?\\d\\.\\d{1,2}\\.\\d{1,2}\\b)+)/g)) {\n        for (const sc of m[1].match(SC)) {\n          if (!wcag[sc]) throw new Error(`docs/02-product-spec.md, ${id}: WCAG ${sc} is not a level A or AA criterion of WCAG 2.2 (apps/docs/data/wcag.mjs)`);\n          if (!cited.has(sc)) cited.set(sc, []);\n          if (!cited.get(sc).some(row => row.id === id)) cited.get(sc).push({ id, criterion: cells[1] });\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return new Map([...cited].sort(([a], [b]) => bySc(a, b)));\n}\n\nconst unreadable = what => { throw new Error(`acceptance checker: output this page cannot read: ${what}`); };\n\n/**\n * The checker's `rows` → row id → { statement, cells: { chartType: { status, reports } } }, each report with\n * the engine it ran on (`engineOf` names it for a report the checker could not: results older than the engine\n * record), whether it counts for this revision, and the commit it was recorded at. Anything outside the\n * checker's documented shape stops the build.\n */\nexport function readRows(rows, { engineOf = () => null, commitOf = () => null } = {}) {\n  if (!Array.isArray(rows)) unreadable('rows is not a list');\n  const out = new Map();\n  for (const row of rows) {\n    if (typeof row?.id !== 'string' || typeof row.statement !== 'string' || row.cells === null || typeof row.cells !== 'object') unreadable(JSON.stringify(row)?.slice(0, 80));\n    const types = Object.keys(row.cells);\n    if (types.length !== CHART_TYPES.length || types.some((type, i) => type !== CHART_TYPES[i])) unreadable(`${row.id}: chart types ${types.join(', ')}`);\n    const cells = {};\n    for (const type of types) {\n      const cell = row.cells[type];\n      if (!STATUSES.includes(cell?.status) || !Array.isArray(cell.reports)) unreadable(`${row.id}/${type}: status ${cell?.status}`);\n      cells[type] = {\n        status: cell.status,\n        reports: cell.reports.map(report => {\n          if (typeof report?.file !== 'string' || !['run', 'size'].includes(report.source) || !Array.isArray(report.results)\n            || report.results.some(r => !RESULTS.includes(r)) || !(report.engine === null || ENGINES.includes(report.engine))\n            || !(report.provenanceError === null || typeof report.provenanceError === 'string')\n            || (report.results.includes('not recorded') && report.provenanceError === null)) {\n            unreadable(`${row.id}/${type}: report ${JSON.stringify(report)?.slice(0, 160)}`);\n          }\n          return {\n            file: report.file, source: report.source, results: report.results,\n            engine: report.source === 'size' ? null : report.engine ?? engineOf(report.file),\n            counted: report.provenanceError === null, why: report.provenanceError, commit: commitOf(report.file),\n          };\n        }),\n      };\n    }\n    out.set(row.id, { statement: row.statement, cells });\n  }\n  return out;\n}\n\nconst json = file => JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));\n\n/**\n * Runs the checker on `root` (the repository; a test passes the checker's own fixtures). Returns what it\n * reported, or its refusal: `refused` holds its error lines, and then no row has a status. `files` are the\n * result files given, with what their sidecars record. By default the result files are acceptance/run.mjs's\n * default ones that exist under `root`.\n */\nexport async function readChecker({ root, matrixFile = path.join(root, 'acceptance/matrix.json'), resultFiles, packIntegrity, sizeTargets } = {}) {\n  const defaults = defaultResultFiles();\n  const engineOf = file => defaults.find(d => d.file === file)?.engine ?? null;\n  const given = resultFiles ?? defaults.map(d => path.join(root, d.file));\n  const present = given.filter(file => fs.existsSync(file)).map(file => path.relative(root, file).split(path.sep).join('/'));\n  const files = present.map(file => {\n    const sidecar = path.join(root, file.replace(/\\.json$/, '.provenance.json'));\n    const provenance = fs.existsSync(sidecar) ? json(sidecar) : null;\n    return { file, provenance, engine: provenance?.engine ?? engineOf(file) };\n  });\n  const label = engine => engine === null ? 'Node' : engineLabel(engine);\n  let result;\n  try {\n    // The checker packs the tree once (npm pack --dry-run) when the matrix cites a size record; a test names the\n    // pack and the targets itself (check()'s `packIntegrity` and `sizeTargets`).\n    const extra = { ...(packIntegrity === undefined ? {} : { packIntegrity }), ...(sizeTargets === undefined ? {} : { sizeTargets }) };\n    result = check({ root, matrixFile, resultFiles: present.map(file => path.join(root, file)), release: true, ...extra });\n  } catch (error) {\n    return { refused: String(error.message).split('\\n').filter(Boolean), rows: new Map(), citations: new Map(), files, label, warnings: [] };\n  }\n  const commitOf = file => files.find(f => f.file === file)?.provenance?.commit ?? null;\n  const rows = readRows(result.rows, { engineOf, commitOf });\n  if (!Array.isArray(result.warnings) || result.warnings.some(w => typeof w !== 'string')) unreadable('warnings is not a list of text');\n  for (const run of result.runs) {\n    const file = path.relative(root, run.file).split(path.sep).join('/');\n    const known = files.find(f => f.file === file);\n    if (known) Object.assign(known, { engine: run.engine ?? known.engine, counted: !run.provenanceError, why: run.provenanceError ?? null });\n  }\n  // The tests each row cites, once the checker has accepted the matrix (so every citation names a real test).\n  const citations = new Map();\n  for (const row of json(matrixFile)) {\n    const seen = new Map();\n    for (const refs of Object.values(row.evidence ?? {})) for (const ref of refs) {\n      const test = ref.title ?? (ref.prefix ? `${ref.prefix}…${ref.atLeast ? ` (at least ${ref.atLeast} cases)` : ''}` : null);\n      const entry = { kind: ref.kind, file: ref.file, test, engine: ref.kind === 'browser' ? ref.engine ?? 'chromium' : null };\n      seen.set(JSON.stringify(entry), entry);\n    }\n    citations.set(row.id, [...seen.values()]);\n  }\n  return { refused: null, rows, citations, files, label, warnings: result.warnings };\n}\n\n/**\n * For one criterion: the checker's verdict over the specification rows that cite it, and what each engine's\n * results said, for this revision only. Every answer is the checker's or \"not recorded\".\n */\nexport function evidenceFor(rows, checker) {\n  const engines = ['chromium', 'firefox', 'webkit', null];\n  const empty = Object.fromEntries(engines.map(engine => [String(engine), 'not recorded']));\n  if (checker.refused) return { verdict: rows.map(row => ({ id: row.id, text: 'not recorded' })), engines: empty };\n  const verdict = [];\n  const seen = new Map(engines.map(engine => [String(engine), { results: new Set(), stale: new Set() }]));\n  for (const { id } of rows) {\n    const row = checker.rows.get(id);\n    if (!row) {\n      verdict.push({ id, text: 'not recorded' });\n      continue;\n    }\n    const groups = new Map();\n    let stale = false;\n    for (const [type, cell] of Object.entries(row.cells)) {\n      if (!groups.has(cell.status)) groups.set(cell.status, []);\n      groups.get(cell.status).push(type);\n      if (cell.status === 'not applicable') continue;\n      for (const report of cell.reports) {\n        if (!report.counted) stale = true;\n        // A size measurement is no engine's: it counts in the checker's verdict only.\n        if (report.source === 'size') continue;\n        const bucket = seen.get(String(report.engine));\n        if (report.counted) for (const value of report.results) bucket.results.add(value);\n        else bucket.stale.add(report.commit ? report.commit.slice(0, 7) : 'unknown');\n      }\n    }\n    verdict.push({\n      id,\n      text: stale ? 'not recorded for this revision' : [...groups].map(([status, types]) => `${status} (${types.join(', ')})`).join('; '),\n    });\n  }\n  const say = ({ results, stale }) => results.size ? [...results].sort().join('/')\n    : stale.size ? `not recorded for this revision (results from ${[...stale].join(', ')})` : 'not recorded';\n  return { verdict, engines: Object.fromEntries([...seen].map(([engine, bucket]) => [engine, say(bucket)])) };\n}\n\n/** The level A and AA criteria the specification does not cite. */\nexport function uncited(cited) {\n  return Object.keys(wcag).filter(sc => !cited.has(sc)).sort(bySc);\n}\n\nexport async function readConformance({ root }) {\n  const spec = fs.readFileSync(path.join(root, 'docs/02-product-spec.md'), 'utf8');\n  const cited = citedCriteria(spec);\n  const checker = await readChecker({ root });\n  const criteria = [...cited].map(([sc, rows]) => ({\n    sc, name: wcag[sc][0], level: wcag[sc][1], rows, ...evidenceFor(rows, checker),\n    tests: rows.flatMap(row => checker.citations.get(row.id) ?? []).filter((t, i, all) => all.findIndex(u => JSON.stringify(u) === JSON.stringify(t)) === i),\n  }));\n  return { criteria, checker, uncited: uncited(cited).map(sc => {\n    const [name, level, kind, why] = wcag[sc];\n    if (!kind || !why) throw new Error(`apps/docs/data/wcag.mjs: WCAG ${sc} is cited by no row of the specification and needs a reason (spec A9)`);\n    return { sc, name, level, kind, why };\n  }) };\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
